  "id": "aboriginal_rock_desert_reggae",
  "name": "Aboriginal rock and desert reggae (Indigenous band music)",
  "family": "rock",
  "lineage": "Band music of Aboriginal communities in Central and northern Australia and in the cities, in which electric rock, country-rock and reggae carry songs in Aboriginal languages and English. The Warumpi Band's Jailanguru Pakarnu (1983), sung in Luritja, is often cited as the first rock song recorded in an Aboriginal language; the Central Australian Aboriginal Media Association (CAAMA) in Alice Springs became a leading label for desert bands, and Coloured Stone, No Fixed Address, Yothu Yindi, Saltwater Band and many community bands followed, some adding didjeridu and clapsticks to the line-up.",
  "recipe": "classic early-1980s aboriginal rock and desert reggae, distorted Rock, regional branch. single lead single track no stacking. single coil electric guitar, electric bass, machine hammered symmetric pattern organized rows full lathed both sides lathed drum kit, traditional some traditions bare wood didgeridoo, clapsticks bilma. treated studio. tube condenser, 500-series boutique pre, brick-wall limiter, dynamic EQ. 24-bit digital plate reverb digital delay pitch shifter.",
  "recipe_chars": 473,
